# Daily check that no Loki rule has gone blind to a host whose logs it is about.
#
# This exists because the offline check could not see the fault it was built
# for, which is the same shape as homelab-check-versions.service above.
# scripts/check_loki_rules.sh boots the pinned Loki image against a throwaway
# config with no data and asks whether the rules PARSE — and that passed for
# five authentication rules that were valid, loaded, evaluated, and matched
# nothing on two of the four monitored hosts, because they selected
# log_type="authlog" while Saruman ships a journal and morpheus ships syslog
# (#261). Parsing and seeing are different questions with the same green
# symptom.
#
# So this asks the live store instead. For each host-scoped rule it compares the
# hosts the rule's own stream selectors reach against the hosts shipping logs at
# all, and for a host it cannot reach it asks whether lines matching what the
# rule hunts exist there anyway. There is no table of which rule should see
# which host — the expectation comes out of the data, because a table like that
# drifts and a drifting table is the defect being hunted (#327).
#
# WHY DAILY AND NOT WEEKLY
#
# #335 proposed weekly and worried that a 7d window against a weekly interval
# barely overlaps, so a gap opening and closing inside a week could be missed.
# The framing was wrong in two ways, and both are worth stating because the
# obvious reading of the window is backwards.
#
# First, a long window does not make the check more sensitive — it makes it
# SLOWER. `reach` counts lines over the window, so a selector that went blind an
# hour ago still shows the host as reached until the last pre-breakage line
# falls out of range. A 7d window hides a new gap for up to a week.
#
# Second, the overlap worry does not apply to this defect class. A rule going
# blind is a configuration fault: it does not heal on its own, it persists until
# somebody fixes it. Tiling 24h windows daily is therefore enough, and a gap
# that genuinely comes and goes inside a day is an agent restart rather than
# something to page about.
#
# Needs Prometheus's neighbour rather than Prometheus: it reads Loki's query API
# over localhost and decrypts nothing. That is why this unit needs neither the
# age key nor a lock, and why the Makefile keeps the target out of `make
# validate` — CI has no log store to ask.
#
# Failure here is a monitoring finding, not an outage. Nothing is broken when it
# exits 1; an alert rule is quietly unable to fire for a host, which is the
# thing #261 opened over and the thing #327 built this to catch.
#
# See homelab-backup-volumes.service for why each directive below is set.
[Unit]
Description=HomeLab: check Loki rules can see every host they are about
Documentation=file:///home/robo/code/Gerrrt/HomeLab/docs/runbooks/schedule-maintenance.md
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target docker.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=robo
Group=robo
WorkingDirectory=/home/robo/code/Gerrrt/HomeLab
Environment=STACK=observability
EnvironmentFile=-/etc/default/homelab-timers
ExecStart=/home/robo/code/Gerrrt/HomeLab/scripts/run-scheduled.sh --job loki-coverage -- /usr/bin/make -C /home/robo/code/Gerrrt/HomeLab check-loki-coverage
TimeoutStartSec=300
Restart=no
Nice=10
ProtectHome=false
ProtectSystem=false
PrivateTmp=true
NoNewPrivileges=true
RestrictSUIDSGID=true
LockPersonality=true
RestrictRealtime=true
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
SyslogIdentifier=homelab-loki-coverage
